            <ArticleTitle>Are all metabolically healthy individuals with obesity at the same risk of diabetes onset?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2615-2623</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/oby.21667</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine the risk of diabetes and the development of an unhealthy status according to metabolic health. To assess the effect of changes in metabolic health among participants with metabolically healthy obesity (MHO) on the risk of diabetes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 4,340 subjects were included. Unhealthy metabolic status was defined as having three or more risk factors of the Adult Treatment Panel-III criteria. A Cox proportional-hazard analysis was conducted to estimate the hazard ratio (HR) of developing diabetes across the change in the metabolic status of subjects with MHO.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After 40,622 person-years of follow-up, the risk of becoming unhealthy was 1.53 times higher for participants with MHO, compared with lean or overweight healthy subjects. A greater risk of diabetes was found in MHO, but it was attributable to those who progressed to an unhealthier status over time: HR of 4.78 (95% CI: 3.38-6.78). The combination of being metabolically unhealthy and obesity heightened the risk of diabetes: HR of 10.09 (95% CI: 4.82-21.55).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The increased risk of diabetes in MHO is attributed to the progression to an unhealthier state. &quot;Healthy obesity&quot; is not a permanent situation but a transitory state.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2016 The Obesity Society.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
